  'about.body':
    "<p><b>Niflaot</b> means “wonders”. The site shows the wonders of the Torah through the eyes of different teachers. Each author and commentator has his own path into the depth of a verse — and each has his own kind of lesson-game. What they share: the answer is never handed to you. You find it yourself, step by step — and then the meaning and the lesson for life open up.</p><h4>The teachers and their lessons</h4><ul><li><b>Rabbi Yitzchak Ginsburgh</b> — articles from the “Niflaot” booklet (Gal Einai): gematria, Kabbalah and Chassidut. <i>A gematria game:</i> add up the letters yourself, find the hidden equation, put the meaning together in a puzzle.</li><li><b>The Baal HaTurim</b> — a classic Torah commentary: gematria, first and last letters of words, observations of the Masorah. <i>Two games:</i> riddles with gematria and letters — and a Memo of “picture — words of the Torah”.</li><li><b>The Lubavitcher Rebbe</b> — talks on the weekly portion (Likkutei Sichot), the “Niflaot of the Rebbe” section in every portion. No gematria here: the Rebbe notices a difficulty in a verse or in Rashi and finds one explanation that resolves all the questions at once. <i>Two games:</i> an investigation — question, clues, the Rebbe’s answer — and cards of “verse — the Rebbe’s explanation”.</li></ul><p>More teachers will come. The lessons are arranged by the weekly Torah portions: each portion has lessons by different authors.</p><h4>In every lesson</h4><ul><li><b>Step-by-step riddles</b> — with hints, and where counting is needed, a “Work it out together” walkthrough.</li><li><b>The lesson revealed</b> — a full retelling of the article, commentary or talk, a summary, a question for yourself and “Hora’ah le-ma’aseh” — a practical takeaway.</li><li><b>Primary sources</b> — the Torah verses, Talmud, midrashim and commentaries the lesson rests on: original and translation side by side, linked to Sefaria.</li><li><b>Read</b> — the same lesson without counting, chapter by chapter: the verse, the commentator’s words in the original with a translation, and the explanation. The Rebbe’s lessons have all the “verse — explanation” pairs in a row and “Remember in rhyme”.</li><li><b>Print</b> — a worksheet with answers for the teacher and cards to cut out: for the table, the classroom and Shabbat.</li><li><b>Three languages</b> — Russian, English and German; play as a guest, no sign-up needed; with an account (a code to your email) your progress is on every device.</li></ul><h4>Also on the site</h4><ul><li><b>Did you know?</b> — all the riddles of the lessons in one feed: guess, open the answer, and find the explanation in the lesson. Any riddle can be shared as a picture.</li><li><b>Gematria of the Day</b> — one word every day: add it up in your head in three tries and share your result.</li><li><b>Remind me every day</b> — if you like, once a day at a time that suits you the site sends a short message from the lessons of the weekly portion: a riddle, a thought or a lesson. A new one every day, nothing on Shabbat. Permission for notifications is asked only after you tap the button.</li><li><b>Offline</b> — the pages you opened are kept in your browser and work without the internet.</li><li><b>No Hebrew needed.</b> Tell the site how you are with Hebrew, and it will show the names of the letters and how the words are read. “The letters in 5 minutes” in the “Mental math” section teaches you to recognize all 22 letters.</li><li><b>Mental math through holy gematria.</b> The “Mental math” section in the menu gives one reliable technique for addition, subtraction, multiplication and division — with examples from the lessons and a three-level trainer.</li><li><b>The AI teacher ✦</b> in the corner of the screen answers right away, at any time: it explains the lesson, a word or a source, shows how to add up the gematria and gives a hint on a riddle without giving the answer away. AI is not a rabbi and can make mistakes: for questions of what to do in practice, ask your rabbi.</li><li><b>A human teacher.</b> Want to learn with a real teacher or ask a personal question — see “Teacher” in the menu.</li><li><b>Gematria step by step and common questions</b> — in “How to play” → “More help”.</li><li><b>The site rests on Shabbat</b> — from Friday evening to Saturday evening in your city.</li><li><b>An account if you want one.</b> Everything is open to guests. With an account — progress on every device, a nickname on the leaderboard, classes with a teacher. Children under 14 need a parent's consent.</li><li><b>No ads, no cookies</b> — the site never asks for your camera, microphone or location.</li></ul><h4>Who it is for</h4><ul><li><b>Parents</b> — to learn Torah together with their children, through play.</li><li><b>Chitas readers</b> — as a continuation of daily learning.</li><li><b>Young prodigies</b> — those who love numbers, puzzles and depth.</li><li><b>Teachers and communities</b> — ready printouts for the classroom and Shabbat, with a QR code to the lesson.</li><li><b>Everyone</b> who wants to see wonders — <i>niflaot</i> — in the Torah and in their own life.</li></ul><h4>Goals</h4><ul><li><b>Learning Torah.</b> Not passive reading but a living search: every connection must be found yourself, and then the meaning stays.</li><li><b>Refining the traits of the soul.</b> Each lesson speaks about concrete traits and leads from understanding to working on oneself.</li><li><b>A clear mind.</b> Counting, comparing and reasoning train attention and memory, and holy words give that training meaning.</li></ul><h4>Tasks</h4><ul><li>To make the depth of the Torah — Rabbi Ginsburgh’s articles, the hints of the classic commentators and the Rebbe’s talks — accessible through play, each in its own genre.</li><li>To base every lesson on primary sources, so that anyone can read them himself.</li><li>To add nothing in the author’s name: the retelling is full and accurate, and the project’s own conclusions are marked as such.</li><li>To publish new lessons regularly by the weekly portions and to translate them into different languages.</li></ul><p>The texts of Rabbi Yitzchak Ginsburgh’s articles belong entirely to him; the full Hebrew text is on pnimi.org.il. The Lubavitcher Rebbe’s talks are retold by the project from the editions of Likkutei Sichot. The Baal HaTurim’s commentary and all primary sources come from the open library Sefaria. The project’s partners are Chabad Haus Wien and JRCV Vienna. Idea and implementation — the Torah project <a href=\"https://mychitas.app\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mychitas.app</a>.</p><p>Site address: <a href=\"https://niflaot.mychitas.app\">niflaot.mychitas.app</a></p>",

